    "narrative": "For LawDog today, our primary offensive engines are **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (81.48) and **Bryce Harper** (80.21). **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** is an elite start against the vulnerable arm of Merrill Kelly (5.13 xERA), and his position in the two-hole provides the optimal environment to capitalize on high-leverage counting stats. Similarly, **Bryce Harper** faces a favorable matchup against Bradish, bolstered by a significant park factor (+16%) and his proven ability to generate elite xwOBA metrics. **Lawrence Butler** also grades out as a top-tier option (74.61), benefiting from a strong platoon advantage against Montero and a favorable venue. These three provide the heavy lifting required to keep us competitive in power and run production.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was keeping **Geraldo Perdomo** on the bench. While his score of 45.25 reflects his utility, he fell victim to a brutal matchup against Williams (2.99 xERA), whose elite suppression skills significantly deflated the projection. We prioritized the higher efficiency of **Masyn Winn** (66.60), who remains a critical start for our SB-scarce roster; his matchup against a soft-tossing Scherzer (5.72 xERA) and his penchant for aggressive base running against a slow delivery make him a high-floor play despite the veteran presence on the mound. \n\nWe must remain cautious with **Dylan Beavers** (53.37), who is currently facing an uphill battle against Wheeler (2.88 xERA). While the park factor is favorable, the combination of an elite catcher\u2019s pop time and Wheeler\u2019s ability to control the running game caps his speed-based upside. Finally, **Cal Raleigh** (48.51) and **Luis Arraez** (48.34) sit right at the margin of our starting threshold. While both are critical to our overall team balance, they face slight platoon or SP-skill headwinds today, and we will be monitoring the game flow closely to ensure these spots don't cannibalize our remaining game caps if their efficiency drops further in-game.",
